Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||02-13-2001||09:26 PM||||I guess someone had better tell them , I was sitting in the car you seen in the magazine about 6 months before anyone even saw or knew what it was.<P>I believe it's car and driver that has a pic of the trunk of the car..no motor.<P>Now the Nissan MID4 concept car was mid engine ..the new Z car is NOT.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/jssig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||BN Boomer||02-13-2001||09:46 PM||||Are you people aware the engine does not necessarily have to be <I>behind</I> the passenger compartment for it to be classified as a mid-engine car? It only has to be somewhere <I>between</I> the front and rear wheel centers. As unconventional as it may sound to those of us accustomed to mid-engine actually meaning somewhere in the back, this means that a design that places the engine forward of the passenger compartment yet behind the front wheels is also considered to be a mid-engine car. I believe the BMW Z8 is another example of this. Check it out for yourselves and let me know if I am wrong.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||3
